# SYNOPSIS

```swift
final class Lock: @unchecked Sendable {
  init()
  func lock()
  func unlock()
  func withLock<R, E: Error>(_ body: () throws(E) -> R) throws(E) -> R
}

enum Futex {
  static let waits: Atomic<Int>
  static let wakes: Atomic<Int>
  static func wait(_ address: UnsafeMutablePointer<UInt32>, current: UInt32,
                   deadline: Int64 = infiniteDeadline) throws(Status)
  static func wake(_ address: UnsafeMutablePointer<UInt32>, count: Int)
}

final class Locked<Value>: @unchecked Sendable {
  init(_ value: Value)
  func withLock<R, E: Error>(_ body: (inout Value) throws(E) -> R)
    throws(E) -> R
}
```

# DESCRIPTION

A `Lock` is a mutual exclusion lock built on futexes.
It is not recursive.
Its state is one word in the object: 0 free, 1 held, 2 held with a thread possibly waiting.
`lock` takes a free lock with one atomic operation and no kernel call.
If the lock is held, it marks the word 2 and sleeps on it with `Futex.wait`, and tries again when woken.
`unlock` sets the word to 0 and, if it was 2, wakes one waiter with `Futex.wake`.
An uncontended lock therefore makes no kernel call in either direction.
`withLock` locks, runs `body`, and unlocks when it ends, by return or by throw, and returns what the body returns.

The lock does not lend priority to the thread holding it.

`Futex.wait` puts the calling thread to sleep while the 32-bit word at `address` holds `current`, until a wake or the monotonic `deadline` (see sys-clock(2)).
If the word already holds something else it throws `badState`; at the deadline, `timedOut`.
`Futex.wake` wakes up to `count` threads sleeping on `address`.
They are `futex_wait(2)` and `futex_wake(2)`.
`waits` and `wakes` count the calls this process has made to each; a lock that is never contended makes none.

`Locked` holds a value that one thread at a time may touch.
`withLock` runs `body` with the value `inout` under a `Lock`.
It exists because the standard library's `Mutex` is not available where Sys runs.

